  <div>
    <h1>How it works</h1>

    <p>
      The predictions are made by an AI model trained on player statistics. It tries to predict how many coins the team will get in a given series of games. Through that it learns each players strengths and weaknesses which lets us put those players on different teams and see the outcomes.
    </p>

    <p>
      But since discourse on AI is a bit spicy right now, I'll answer a few questions you might have.
    </p>

    <h2>Is this model bad for the environment?</h2>

    <p>
      In short, no. Its a very small model, about 1/1,000,000,000th the size of an LLM like ChatGPT or Claude. Its small enough that it runs in your browser with almost no load time.
    </p>

    <p>
      In terms of energy use, it trained in about 10 minutes on a 5 year old laptop. You've probably used more energy playing Minecraft.
    </p>

    <p>
      In terms of water use, I drank a coke while I made it... so theres that.
    </p>

    <h2>How was the data obtained?</h2>

    <p>
      The data was taken from the <a href="https://mcchampionship.fandom.com/wiki/MC_Championship_Wiki">MCC wiki</a> which is licensed under the <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/">Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike license</a>. A permissive license that lets you remix, transform, and build upon the content.
    </p>

    <p>
      Some data also comes from the MCC Island API.
    </p>

    <p>
      I wont accept any data on players that isn't already publically available. Don't dox people its not nice.
    </p>

    <h2>Will this AI model cause the downfall of humanity?</h2>

    <p>
      No, I don't think so... Unless the fate of humanity depends on predictions of fictional Minecraft
      events for some reason.
    </p>

    <h2>Should I use the output to place bets or participate in prediction markets?</h2>

    <p>God no. This is a hobby project purely made for fun and is not fit for use for any purpose.</p>

    <h2>Why is the sky blue?</h2>

    <p>I don't see how that is relevant, but said I would answer your questions, sooooo... Its Rayleigh scattering.</p>

